WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:06.470
One of my arguments has been that Kimmy Schmidt
is a sixties gimmick sitcom that's been updated

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.050
for the 2010s.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:15.450
The premise of Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt is
that Kimmy Schmidt, herself, was in a bunker

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.670
for many many years.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:20.699
After she was rescued she decides that she's
going to stay in New York City.

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:26.150
She's gone from this world of terrible terrible
trauma to a world where everything is bursting

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.740
with new opportunity and the color represents
that in many ways.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.710
Television has had color since the 1960s but
it's one of those things where a lot of shows

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.670
haven't had time to really build out color
pallets.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.239
Now we've had some great shows with lots of
color in them.

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:43.149
Pushing Daisies is one that comes to mind.

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:45.739
There's a show where everything in it was
color coordinated.

00:00:45.739 --> 00:00:48.469
That's from creator Brian Fuller who frequently
does such things.

00:00:48.469 --> 00:00:53.010
He also does tremendous work with color on
Hannibal where he uses rich blood reds to

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.899
accent the scenes of death and horror.

00:00:56.899 --> 00:01:01.409
Kimmy Schmidt definitely wants to signal to
you that this is taking place in a weird heightened

00:01:01.409 --> 00:01:02.409
world.

00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:08.910
Kimmy and Titus the roomates, they're both
often colored in just very bright accent colors

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:13.180
that stand out from the rest of the scene
or if you look carefully are often color coordinated

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.610
with sets and with the props around them.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.850
Another example would be Jacqueline who is
the woman that Kimmy works for.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:25.890
She's very often in dressed in whites, beiges,
things that sort of blend in with her environment.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:31.380
She is a woman who is from the very rich upper
class world and so she, of course, doesn't

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.990
want to rock the boat too much and her color
reflects that.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:39.310
And finally the guy that kidnapped Kimmy is
always in very white and black tones.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:43.930
This sort of represents, kind of, his religious
world view but it also represents that fact

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:47.850
that he's trying to drag Kimmy back to a world
of monochrome.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:52.500
I think episode 6, in general, is really interesting
in terms of color coordination.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.670
When Kimmy is at school where she's trying
to get her GED from a teacher who just doesn't

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:01.910
care, he's again, dressed in sort of the colors
of white of beige.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:06.630
The monochrome is the hue of the people who
are trying to keep Kimmy down on this show.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.859
Where as Kimmy is dressed in a very bright
green that contrasts well with the green of

00:02:10.859 --> 00:02:15.549
the blackboards and with everybody around
her.

00:02:15.549 --> 00:02:19.959
This is a show that, I think, has a couple
of small issues here and there but it's definitely

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:23.760
a show that, by the end of season one, I was
very much in to.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.540
I just really want to spend more time in this
world because it's so interesting and colorful

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:38.939
and unlike anything else on television.

